userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using SVG.js.

CONCEPT: Enzyme specificity arises from the complementary fit between substrate and active site — either as a rigid pre-formed pocket (lock-and-key) or as a flexible pocket that reshapes itself upon binding (induced fit).
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): Specificity does not require a rigid pocket — in induced fit the enzyme earns its complementary shape only after the correct substrate arrives, so the fit is a consequence of binding rather than a precondition for it.
DEMO STEPS: 5 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

FULL BLUEPRINT:
{
  "concept": "Enzyme specificity arises from the complementary fit between substrate and active site — either as a rigid pre-formed pocket (lock-and-key) or as a flexible pocket that reshapes itself upon binding (induced fit).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Green enzyme body: large rounded trapezoidal blob with dark olive outline and pale yellow-green fill, flat-ish bottom, domed top with a notched cleft",
    "Active site cleft: V/W-shaped notch on the top surface of the enzyme, with two small colored contact patches (a rounded pink/tan pocket on the left, a pointed tan pocket on the right)",
    "Substrate A: purple rounded shape with a flat left edge and a small rectangular peg protruding downward, tipped with a pink/tan contact region",
    "Substrate B: purple rounded shape with a pointed/tapered peg protruding downward, tipped with a tan contact region",
    "Bound complex rendering: both substrates seated in the cleft, joined along a thick black vertical seam where their flat faces meet",
    "Black wedge arrows pointing from the substrates down into the active site",
    "Leader lines from the 'Substrates' label to each purple molecule",
    "Text label: 'Substrates'",
    "Text label: 'Active site is proper shape' (lock-and-key state)",
    "Text label: 'Active site changes to fit' (induced-fit state)",
    "Text label: 'Substrate complex formed'",
    "Text label: 'Enzyme' beneath each enzyme body",
    "Panel captions: '(a) Lock-and-key model' and '(b) Induced fit model'",
    "Dashed outline ghost of the enzyme's original (unbound) active-site contour, shown overlaid during induced-fit binding to reveal the conformational change",
    "Small annotation callout marking the region of conformational change in the induced-fit model"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "modelToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That both models explain specificity, but only one requires the enzyme itself to change shape — the rigid pocket versus the adaptive pocket.",
      "label": "Binding model",
      "options": [
        "Lock-and-key",
        "Induced fit"
      ],
      "default": "Lock-and-key"
    },
    {
      "id": "bindingProgress",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That binding is a continuous approach, and in induced fit the active site contour deforms progressively as the substrate nears, while in lock-and-key it stays fixed at every value.",
      "label": "Binding progress (substrate approach → complex)",
      "range": [
        0,
        1,
        0.01
      ],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "playBinding",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "The dynamic sense of docking: substrates descending into the cleft and, in induced fit, the enzyme closing around them.",
      "label": "Play substrate docking",
      "loop": false
    },
    {
      "id": "showGhostOutline",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Exactly how much the active site moved, by comparing the bound contour against the unbound contour in the same frame.",
      "label": "Show original active-site outline",
      "default": false
    },
    {
      "id": "partLabels",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Which region is the enzyme, the active site, the substrate, and the complementary contact patch that determines specificity."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Free enzyme and substrates",
      "narration": "A green enzyme sits below two purple substrate molecules. Hover any part to name it: enzyme body, active site cleft, and the small colored contact patches that must match.",
      "state": {
        "modelToggle": "Lock-and-key",
        "bindingProgress": 0,
        "showGhostOutline": false
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lock-and-key: the pocket is already right",
      "narration": "Drag binding progress forward. The substrates descend and slot in, but trace the cleft outline — it never moves. The active site is a rigid, pre-formed shape.",
      "state": {
        "modelToggle": "Lock-and-key",
        "bindingProgress": 1,
        "showGhostOutline": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Switch to induced fit, unbound",
      "narration": "Now the same enzyme is drawn with a shallower, more open cleft — it does not yet match the substrates. Under lock-and-key logic, nothing should bind here.",
      "state": {
        "modelToggle": "Induced fit",
        "bindingProgress": 0,
        "showGhostOutline": false
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Induced fit: the pocket closes in",
      "narration": "Sweep the slider again. As the substrates approach, the enzyme's surface deforms and wraps around them — the dashed ghost shows where the cleft used to be.",
      "state": {
        "modelToggle": "Induced fit",
        "bindingProgress": 1,
        "showGhostOutline": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare the two complexes",
      "narration": "Play the docking animation in each mode back to back. Both end in a tight substrate complex — the difference is whether the enzyme had to move to get there.",
      "state": {
        "modelToggle": "Induced fit",
        "bindingProgress": 1,
        "showGhostOutline": false
      }
    }
